---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import { installChannels } from '../data/install-channels';
import { links, REPO } from '../data/site';

const cloneCmd = `git clone ${REPO}.git\ncd hq`;
const rustupCmd = "curl --proto '=https' --tlsv1.2 -sSf https://sh.rustup.rs | sh";
const linuxDeps = 'sudo apt install -y build-essential pkg-config perl libssl-dev';
const buildCmd = 'cargo build --release -p hq-cli';
const pathCmd = 'install -m 755 target/release/hq "$HOME/.local/bin/hq"';
const firstRun = 'hq install   # scaffold the vault, seed the soul, write config\nhq env       # set LLM API keys interactively\nhq doctor    # verify the setup\nhq chat      # start an interactive session';
const webBuild = 'cd apps/hq-web && bun install && bun run build\ncp -r dist/client ../../web/dist\ncd ../..\nhq start all   # API, WebSocket and the PWA on :5678\nhq pwa         # open http://localhost:5678';
const mcpCmd = 'hq mcp install --target claude-code\nhq mcp status';

const provision = "ssh <ssh-host> 'bash -s' < deploy/setup-vps.sh";
const serverDeps = 'sudo apt install -y bubblewrap minisign';
const serverInstall = 'sudo deploy/install.sh --repo CalvinMagezi/hq --channel stable --pubkey release/minisign.pub';
const updateOps = 'hq update --check                  # exit 10 means an update is waiting\nhq update --apply --dry-run        # verify the release, change nothing\nhq update --apply\nhq update --pin <version> --apply  # exact version, downgrades allowed\nhq update --rollback               # previous binary and web files';

const tsCaddy = 'sudo cp deploy/Caddyfile.pwa /etc/caddy/Caddyfile.pwa\necho "import Caddyfile.pwa" | sudo tee -a /etc/caddy/Caddyfile\nsudo caddy validate --config /etc/caddy/Caddyfile && sudo systemctl reload caddy';
const tsUp = 'sudo tailscale up';
const tsServe = 'sudo tailscale serve --bg --https=8443 http://127.0.0.1:4749\ntailscale serve status';
const tsConfig = 'web_allowed_origins:\n  - https://hq.example.ts.net:8443\nweb_auth_token: "<a long random value>"';
const tsOff = 'sudo tailscale serve --https=8443 off';

const npx = installChannels.npx;
const curl = installChannels.curl;
const brew = installChannels.brew;
const docker = installChannels.docker;
---

<Layout
  title="Install Agent HQ"
  description="Install Agent HQ with a curl script, Homebrew, npx or Docker, set up a server with signed releases, and reach the web UI over Tailscale."
  path="/install/"
>
  <section class="band">
    <div class="container section">
      <span class="eyebrow">Install</span>
      <h1>Install Agent HQ</h1>
      <p class="lede prose">
        There are seven working ways in. The quickest is one command that downloads a verified release. Building from source runs on Linux and macOS. The server path
        installs signed releases and updates itself, and it is Linux only. Tailscale is the
        optional way to open the web UI from your phone.
      </p>
      <nav aria-label="On this page">
        <ul class="toc">
          <li><a href="#script">Install script</a></li>
          <li><a href="#homebrew">Homebrew</a></li>
          <li><a href="#npx">npx installer</a></li>
          <li><a href="#docker">Docker</a></li>
          <li><a href="#windows">Windows (WSL2)</a></li>
          <li><a href="#source">Build from source</a></li>
          <li><a href="#server">Server with signed releases</a></li>
          <li><a href="#tailscale">Remote access over Tailscale</a></li>
          <li><a href="#maintain">Updating and uninstalling</a></li>
          <li><a href="#soon">Not available yet</a></li>
        </ul>
      </nav>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="container">
    {
      curl.available && (
        <section class="doc-section" id="script" aria-labelledby="script-h">
          <h2 id="script-h">{curl.title}</h2>
          <div class="prose">
            <p>
              The quickest way in, with nothing to install first. It detects your platform (Linux x86_64 or
              ARM, or an Apple Silicon Mac), downloads the latest stable release, verifies its minisign signature
              and SHA-256 checksum against the project key, and installs <code>hq</code> into
              <code>~/.local/bin</code> and the web UI into <code>~/.local/share/agent-hq/web</code>. After
              <code>hq start all</code> the app is at <code>http://localhost:5678</code>. It checks signatures with the <code>minisign</code> tool or with OpenSSL;
              on a Mac, install one of them first (<code>brew install minisign</code>). Read the script before you
              run it: <a href="/install.sh">install.sh</a>.
            </p>
            <CodeBlock id="curl-cmd" label="Shell" code={curl.command} />
            <p>Then run <code>hq install</code>, add an API key with <code>hq env</code>, and check with <code>hq doctor</code>.</p>
          </div>
        </section>
      )
    }
    {
      brew.available && (
        <section class="doc-section" id="homebrew" aria-labelledby="homebrew-h">
          <h2 id="homebrew-h">{brew.title}</h2>
          <div class="prose">
            <p>
              For macOS on Apple Silicon and Linux. The tap installs the signed stable release of
              <code>hq</code> and upgrades with <code>brew upgrade agent-hq</code>. The formula is regenerated
              every six hours from the signed release manifest, and its checksums are written only after the
              signature verifies. It installs the binary only; the install script above also installs the web UI.
            </p>
            <CodeBlock id="brew-cmd" label="Shell" code={brew.command} />
          </div>
        </section>
      )
    }
    {
      npx.available && (
        <section class="doc-section" id="npx" aria-labelledby="npx-h">
          <h2 id="npx-h">{npx.title}</h2>
          <div class="prose">
            <p>
              Needs Node 18.17 or newer, which stock Ubuntu 22.04 does not have; use the install script there. It downloads the latest stable release for your platform, checks the
              minisign signature and the SHA-256 checksum against the project key, and installs
              <code>hq</code> into <code>~/.local/bin</code>. On a platform without a prebuilt binary it
              builds from source with cargo.
            </p>
            <CodeBlock id="npx-cmd" label="npx" code={npx.command} />
            <p>Then run <code>hq install</code>, add an API key with <code>hq env</code>, and check with <code>hq doctor</code>.</p>
          </div>
        </section>
      )
    }
    {
      docker.available && (
        <section class="doc-section" id="docker" aria-labelledby="docker-h">
          <h2 id="docker-h">{docker.title}</h2>
          <div class="prose">
            <p>
              A multi-arch image (Linux x86_64 and ARM) assembled from the signed release, published to the GitHub
              Container Registry. The server inside binds a public address, so HQ requires a web token: the first
              run prints a link that carries it, once. The port is published on <code>127.0.0.1</code> only. Add
              <code>-e HQ_OPENROUTER_API_KEY=...</code> (or another provider key) to give it a model. State lives in the
              <code>hq-data</code> volume. Read the <a href="https://github.com/CalvinMagezi/hq/blob/main/docs/DOCKER.md">Docker guide</a>
              for the sandbox note, backups and Tailscale.
            </p>
            <CodeBlock id="docker-cmd" label="Shell" code={docker.command} />
            <p>Then run <code>docker logs hq</code> and open the link it prints.</p>
          </div>
        </section>
      )
    }
    <section class="doc-section" id="windows" aria-labelledby="windows-h">
      <h2 id="windows-h">Windows</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          There is no native Windows build yet. HQ runs well inside WSL2, which is a normal Ubuntu: open
          an Ubuntu terminal and use the install script above, exactly as on Linux. Open the web UI from
          your Windows browser at <code>http://localhost:5678</code>. Install bubblewrap
          (<code>sudo apt install bubblewrap</code>) so the agent's shell tool has its sandbox.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="doc-section" id="source" aria-labelledby="source-h">
      <h2 id="source-h">{installChannels.cargo.title}</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          You need git, a recent stable Rust toolchain and at least one LLM API key from OpenRouter,
          Anthropic or Google AI. Edition 2024 needs Rust 1.85 or newer, and CI pins 1.98.1. The web UI
          also needs <a href={links.bun}>bun</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li>
          <p>Install Rust with <a href={links.rustup}>rustup</a>, if you do not have it.</p>
          <CodeBlock id="src-rustup" label="Install Rust" code={rustupCmd} />
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>Clone the repository.</p>
          <CodeBlock id="src-clone" label="Clone" code={cloneCmd} />
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>On Debian or Ubuntu, install the build dependencies first.</p>
          <CodeBlock id="src-deps" label="Linux build dependencies" code={linuxDeps} />
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>Build the release binary. It is about 40 MB.</p>
          <CodeBlock id="src-build" label="Build" code={buildCmd} />
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>Put it on your PATH. Make sure <code>~/.local/bin</code> is on it.</p>
          <CodeBlock id="src-path" label="Install the binary" code={pathCmd} />
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>Run first-run setup.</p>
          <CodeBlock id="src-first" label="First run" code={firstRun} />
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Optional: build the web UI and serve it. The server serves <code>web/dist</code> next to
            the vault, or the directory in <code>web_static_dir</code>.
          </p>
          <CodeBlock id="src-web" label="Web UI" code={webBuild} />
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>Optional: connect an MCP client such as Claude Code.</p>
          <CodeBlock id="src-mcp" label="MCP" code={mcpCmd} />
        </li>
      </ol>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Chat relays need a bot token in <code>~/.hq/config.yaml</code>. Then run
          <code>hq pair</code> and send the code from your own account, so the bot knows who the owner
          is. The <a href={links.readme}>README</a> has the config keys.
        </p>
        <p>
          Agent shell commands need a sandbox. On macOS that is <code>sandbox-exec</code>, which ships
          with the system. On Linux install bubblewrap, or HQ refuses to run model-written bash.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="doc-section" id="server" aria-labelledby="server-h">
      <h2 id="server-h">{installChannels.server.title}</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          For an always-on instance on a Debian or Ubuntu VPS. The installer sets up a systemd timer
          that pulls minisign-verified releases, swaps the binary, checks <code>/health</code> and
          rolls back if the check fails. It runs from a checkout of the repository on the server,
          because it installs the unit files from there. Server installs run on Ubuntu 22.04 or newer, on x86_64 or ARM.
        </p>
      </div>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li>
          <p>
            Provision the box once from your machine. This installs Tailscale, Caddy, ufw and
            <code>gh</code>, creates an unprivileged <code>hq</code> user and writes a starter config
            with both chat relays disabled. <code>&lt;ssh-host&gt;</code> is an SSH alias for your
            server.
          </p>
          <CodeBlock id="srv-provision" label="Provision" code={provision} />
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>On the server, clone the repository (same commands as above).</p>
          <CodeBlock id="srv-clone" label="Clone" code={cloneCmd} />
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Install bubblewrap, which the default sandbox mode needs, and the <code>minisign</code>
            tool, which the installer uses to check the first download.
          </p>
          <CodeBlock id="srv-deps" label="Dependencies" code={serverDeps} />
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Run the installer with the official release key from the repository,
            <a href={links.officialKey}><code>release/minisign.pub</code></a>. It creates the user and
            directories, installs the units, writes <code>/etc/hq/update.conf</code> and the key,
            bootstraps the first binary, runs the first signature-verified update and enables the
            timer. It is safe to run again.
          </p>
          <CodeBlock id="srv-install" label="Install" code={serverInstall} />
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>Day to day.</p>
          <CodeBlock id="srv-ops" label="Updates" code={updateOps} />
        </li>
      </ol>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          <code>deploy/install.sh</code> does not install Herdr. If you want coding-agent sessions on
          the server, follow the local setup in <a href={links.herdrHarness}>the Herdr docs</a>. Pick one
          deploy path per host: the updater owns the binary and web files, so do not also build on the
          server. Full walkthrough: <a href={links.deployReadme}>deploy/README.md</a>. To check a
          release by hand, see <a href="/security/#verify">verifying a release</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="doc-section" id="tailscale" aria-labelledby="ts-h">
      <h2 id="ts-h">{installChannels.tailscale.title}</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Optional. This puts the web UI on your tailnet over HTTPS, so your phone can reach it and the
          public internet cannot. HQ stays on loopback, Caddy listens on <code>127.0.0.1:4749</code>,
          and <a href={links.tailscaleServe}>tailscale serve</a> terminates TLS. HTTPS certificates
          must be enabled for your tailnet. Run these on the server, from the repository checkout.
          <code>hq.example.ts.net</code> stands for your server's own MagicDNS name.
        </p>
      </div>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li>
          <p>Join the tailnet.</p>
          <CodeBlock id="ts-up" label="Tailscale up" code={tsUp} />
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>Add the loopback Caddy bridge.</p>
          <CodeBlock id="ts-caddy" label="Caddy" code={tsCaddy} />
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Serve it on an alternate HTTPS port. Port 443 is usually taken by a public Caddyfile.
          </p>
          <CodeBlock id="ts-serve" label="Serve" code={tsServe} />
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            List every origin you open the UI from in <code>/opt/hq/config.yaml</code>, and set a web
            token. Without the origin you get a 403 about an unrecognised Host header. Restart HQ
            afterwards.
          </p>
          <CodeBlock id="ts-config" label="config.yaml" code={tsConfig} />
        </li>
      </ol>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>To turn it off again, run this. Never publish <code>/ws</code> or <code>/api</code> on a public address.</p>
        <CodeBlock id="ts-off" label="Tailscale serve off" code={tsOff} />
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="doc-section" id="maintain" aria-labelledby="maintain-h">
      <h2 id="maintain-h">Updating and uninstalling</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Each channel updates the way it installed. Your notes and config are never touched by an update.
          <code>hq update</code> only manages servers set up with the server installer; on any other install it
          tells you the right command.
        </p>
        <ul class="facts">
          <li><strong>Install script or npx.</strong> Update by running the script again. Uninstall:
            <code>rm ~/.local/bin/hq</code> and <code>rm -rf ~/.local/share/agent-hq</code>.</li>
          <li><strong>Homebrew.</strong> <code>brew upgrade agent-hq</code>. Uninstall: <code>brew uninstall agent-hq</code>.</li>
          <li><strong>Docker.</strong> <code>docker pull ghcr.io/calvinmagezi/hq</code>, then recreate the container.
            Uninstall: <code>docker rm -f hq</code>; <code>docker volume rm hq-data</code> also deletes your data.</li>
          <li><strong>Server.</strong> It updates itself from signed releases every ten minutes. To remove it, back up
            <code>/opt/hq/.vault</code> first, then <code>systemctl disable --now hq herdr hq-update.timer</code> and delete
            the unit files, <code>/usr/local/bin/hq</code>, <code>/etc/hq</code> and <code>/opt/hq</code>.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Your vault and config live in <code>~/.vault</code> and <code>~/.hq</code> on a personal install. Keep or delete them
          as you like; nothing above removes them.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="doc-section" id="soon" aria-labelledby="soon-h">
      <h2 id="soon-h">Not available yet</h2>
      <div class="callout prose">
        <p>
          These do not exist today. We list them so you do not go looking, and so you know they are not
          promised for any date.
        </p>
        <ul class="facts">
          <li>Prebuilt binaries for Intel Macs, and a native Windows build. Windows runs HQ through WSL2 today.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</Layout>
